figdown 0.3 bitfield
# `DRAWN-ANNOTATION-FORM`: `field` REFUSES `note=`, and does so at EVERY version.
# One document, two sections, the two versions that matter — the message is
# the same string in both, which is the whole point of the fixture.
#
# WHY A DEDICATED MESSAGE RATHER THAN THE GENERIC ONE. `field` is listed in
# DIRECTIVE_OPTS as taking `note` for exactly one reason: so that the refusal
# is REASONED instead of falling through to `field does not take note=` (the
# answer the seven directives in case 804 get). The distinction it has to
# state is not "wrong directive", it is AUDIENCE — `description=` is not a
# replacement for `note=` here, it reaches a different reader — and a generic
# message cannot say that.
#
# WHY THE REFUSAL IS VERSION-INDEPENDENT. Section 2 declares `figdown 0.1`
# and gets the SAME message, not the version gate of case 805. If `field`
# fell through to `belowOptVersion` under an old header, the engine would
# tell a `figdown 0.1` bitfield author to raise their header to 0.3 — sending
# them to a version that still refuses them. The engine checks `field` FIRST
# and unconditionally so that never happens: ONE ERROR PER LINE, and it is
# the true one.
#
# The evidence behind the refusal: the bitfield genre already has
# `description=` for machine-facing prose, and no measured figure needs a
# DRAWN per-field aside. Granting one directive both keys with nothing asking
# for it spends the distinction before anyone needs it.
bitfield hdr "Header, declared 0.3" word=32 numbering=msb0
field "A" 16 note="refused under 0.3 — raising the header does not help"
field "B" 16 description="the key a field does take"

figdown 0.1 bitfield
# Section 2: the identical line under the OLDEST header. Same message.
bitfield old "Header, declared 0.1" word=32 numbering=msb0
field "C" 16 note="refused under 0.1 — the same message, not a version gate"
field "D" 16 present="C = 1"
